## Design Decisions (Based on Your Answers)

### ✅ Internet Required Only ONCE — For Initial Registration

**How it works in simple words:**

| Scenario | What Happens |
|----------|-------------|
| 🆕 **First time** app opens on a new device | Must have internet. Checks Firebase. If authorized → saves permanently |
| 📴 **Every time after** (with or without internet) | App opens normally from cache. **No internet needed ever again** |
| 🔌 Client's internet goes down for weeks/months | **No problem.** App keeps working. No disturbance at all |
| 🗑️ Client uninstalls and reinstalls app | Cache is cleared → needs internet again for one-time re-verification |

> [!NOTE]
> This means the cache is **permanent** — it survives reboots, offline periods, anything. Only uninstalling the app clears it.

### ✅ Revocation Works Opportunistically (Background Check)

Even though internet is only required once, revocation still works:

- Every time the app opens, **IF internet happens to be available**, it silently re-checks Firebase in the background
- If you've set `active: false` → the cache gets updated → client is locked out
- If no internet → the app just opens normally from cache, no delay, no disturbance
- The background check has a **3-second timeout** — if Firebase doesn't respond fast, the app just opens from cache. Zero friction.

**To revoke a device:**
1. Set `active: false` in Firebase Console
2. Next time the client opens the app **while connected to internet** → locked out
3. If they have no internet, they can still use the app (but the moment they get internet and reopen, they're locked out)

